  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 146 PM EST Mon Nov 9 2026\n\nCurrently, troughing is centered to our northeast with ridging \nbuilding to our west. Surface high pressure remains in place across \nthe region, centered near the southern Mississippi River Valley. \nThis will continue to produce dry conditions during peak heating \nwith height rises allowing highs to get further into the 60s. With \nsubsidence, another cool night in the 30s can be expected for most \nplaces. On Tuesday, flow becomes more quasi-zonal with additional \nheight rises warming daytime highs to around 70 for most places. By \nWednesday, a shortwave will eject out of the northern Great Plains \nwith a deepening surface low progressing towards the Ohio River \nValley. This will lead to increasing southerly flow and moisture \nadvection sufficient for a return of rain chances area-wide into \nThursday morning. \n\nThe surface low then progresses to our northeast through the day \nwith the associated cold front progressing off to the east during \nthe day with a shift to northwesterly flow. This will lead to more \nfocus along the higher elevations for orographic lift and decreasing \ncoverage elsewhere. By early evening, 850mb temperatures will drop \nto freezing with 20 to 25 kts of flow and moisture in the lower \nlevels, marking a transition to snow in the mountains. By Friday \nmorning, 850mb flow will remain largely unchanged with \ntemperatures at this level falling to near -5 Celsius and \nadditional moisture. The first widespread high elevation snow is \nlikely, especially across the highest peaks. Based on these \nindications, well below normal temperatures are expected area-wide\non Friday with a few sprinkles or flurries possible. Low-level \nmoisture is slow to exit the higher elevations on Friday night \nwith light snow showers possibly lingering into Saturday. The main\nquestion at this point will be how much moisture lingers as the \ndepth of cold air and low- level flow are both very confident. \nRegardless, impacts will likely be limited to the higher \nelevations. Troughing will remain in place through the weekend \nwith approaching Canadian high pressure leading to colder and dry \nconditions, especially in the mornings. Lows will likely be below \nfreezing area-wide with some places falling deep into the 20s.",
